The input x and ouput y are not just guessing.
We write how many examples we want the NN to memorize in x and what it wants to memorize it as in y.
In adding two numbers we have only one solution so: (1D)
    - [1,0,1,1] ---> x size = 4 #2+3 represented
    - [1,1,1] ---> y size = 3 #5 represented

But if we want to let the NN memorize multilple examples at once we do: (2D)
    - [
        [1,0,1,1], #2+3
        [0,1,0,1]  #1+1
    ] ----> x size = 4

    -[
        [1,1,1], #5
        [0,1,0]  #2
    ] -----> y size = 3

    but after training the data even if single output is passed the 2D address only returns the answer of the part it recognize except of zero array of the previous data training format.

    # this is due to matrix multiplication
    # also the layers only depend upon howmany features a example has not the number of examples passed throgh it.